WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.767 --> 00:00:02.068
-Our next guest is

00:00:02.068 --> 00:00:04.003
an Academy Award-winning
producer, director, and actor.

00:00:04.003 --> 00:00:06.839
His documentary series "Beckham"
is streaming now on Netflix.

00:00:06.839 --> 00:00:08.107
Let's take a look.

00:00:08.107 --> 00:00:10.075
-You know, playing
at the national team stadium,

00:00:10.075 --> 00:00:12.579
playing at Wembley...

00:00:12.579 --> 00:00:16.216
it's a very emotional
moment for me,

00:00:16.216 --> 00:00:18.651
because my dad always used to
take me to Wembley

00:00:18.651 --> 00:00:20.253
to watch England play.

00:00:20.253 --> 00:00:23.757
That was really what
my childhood was all about.

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:31.664
-So seeing my son come out
for the first time at Wembley,

00:00:31.664 --> 00:00:35.401
I must admit, I had a --
I had a little tear.

00:00:35.401 --> 00:00:38.604
-Please welcome to the show
Fisher Stevens, everybody.

00:00:38.604 --> 00:00:40.606
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:40.606 --> 00:00:54.454 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:54.454 --> 00:00:56.089
Welcome to the show, Fisher.

00:00:56.089 --> 00:00:58.824
-Thank you.
So great to be here.

00:00:58.824 --> 00:01:00.393
-I'm so happy to have you.

00:01:00.393 --> 00:01:02.194
I know you are
a proud member of SAG,

00:01:02.194 --> 00:01:04.497
and to respect
the rules of the strike,

00:01:04.497 --> 00:01:06.832
we're not going to talk about
any of your acting work,

00:01:06.832 --> 00:01:08.934
but I am just going to
vaguely say

00:01:08.934 --> 00:01:12.004
you were in an excellent show
that ended this year,

00:01:12.004 --> 00:01:13.639
and you were excellent in it.

00:01:13.639 --> 00:01:15.742
-Thank you.
-Yes. Keep it vague.

00:01:15.742 --> 00:01:18.244
-Thank you.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:18.244 --> 00:01:20.513
I loved being on that show

00:01:20.513 --> 00:01:23.015
and doing that show
and sad it ended.

00:01:23.015 --> 00:01:24.550
-There we go.
I think that's perfect.

00:01:24.550 --> 00:01:25.852
That show.

00:01:25.852 --> 00:01:29.756
You are an Oscar-winning
documentary filmmaker,

00:01:29.756 --> 00:01:31.790
and this came across your plate,

00:01:31.790 --> 00:01:34.293
this chance to do a documentary
about David Beckham.

00:01:34.293 --> 00:01:36.629
You weren't necessarily
a giant fan of his.

00:01:36.629 --> 00:01:37.763
This wasn't the sort of project

00:01:37.763 --> 00:01:39.365
you were out there
trying to land.

00:01:39.365 --> 00:01:43.002
-Right, I actually --
I got a call

00:01:43.002 --> 00:01:45.838
on my way to that show, to work,

00:01:45.838 --> 00:01:50.844
and it was from my old pal
Leonardo DiCaprio's office

00:01:50.844 --> 00:01:52.045
saying --

00:01:52.045 --> 00:01:54.813
-What a funny way
to end that sentence.

00:01:54.813 --> 00:01:57.616
"My dear friend
Leo DiCaprio's office."

00:01:57.616 --> 00:02:00.620
-We had made -- We made
some documentaries together,

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:04.323
and the office said,
"Leo suggested you

00:02:04.323 --> 00:02:06.359
to direct David Beckham's
documentary,

00:02:06.359 --> 00:02:07.560
and David wants to talk to you."

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:10.496
And I was like, "Me?
David Beckham?"

00:02:10.496 --> 00:02:12.932
I really wasn't that excited.

00:02:12.932 --> 00:02:14.600
I love football, soccer.

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:18.971
I really got into it after he
left England and went to Madrid

00:02:18.971 --> 00:02:21.941
and just thought,
Brand Beckham, Posh Spice,

00:02:21.941 --> 00:02:23.743
wasn't really that interested.

00:02:23.743 --> 00:02:25.811
And then I went to work
on that show,

00:02:25.811 --> 00:02:28.447
and the people that worked on it
are all English,

00:02:28.447 --> 00:02:29.782
and they're like, "You have to.

00:02:29.782 --> 00:02:31.650
He's a legend.
She's amazing.

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:33.552
You don't know
what the guy went through.

00:02:33.552 --> 00:02:34.753
Great story."

00:02:34.753 --> 00:02:37.056
And I did a Zoom with him,

00:02:37.056 --> 00:02:38.591
and he was very different
than I thought,

00:02:38.591 --> 00:02:41.127
and then met him
and his wife for dinner,

00:02:41.127 --> 00:02:43.296
and they were amazing, much --

00:02:43.296 --> 00:02:46.031
We had a few drinks,
so it was very loose,

00:02:46.031 --> 00:02:50.436
and they said, "Oh, Fisher,
you don't know our story.

00:02:50.436 --> 00:02:52.171
You don't know our story."

00:02:52.171 --> 00:02:55.808
And I got excited,
and that was it, that was it is.

00:02:55.808 --> 00:03:00.145
-You know, being a football fan
and especially that era,

00:03:00.145 --> 00:03:02.348
that World Cup that sort of ends
the first episode,

00:03:02.348 --> 00:03:05.151
that's when I really feel like
I fell in love with the sport.

00:03:05.151 --> 00:03:06.385
So I was so excited for that.

00:03:06.385 --> 00:03:09.121
This is a love story,
though, man.

00:03:09.121 --> 00:03:11.524
It is so wonderful
to watch this couple

00:03:11.524 --> 00:03:13.126
and the way they talk
about each other

00:03:13.126 --> 00:03:15.027
and the way they open up to you.

00:03:15.027 --> 00:03:16.896
Do you feel like
it was easy for them,

00:03:16.896 --> 00:03:19.431
or were they hesitant at all?

00:03:19.431 --> 00:03:21.067
-Well, I asked them --

00:03:21.067 --> 00:03:22.969
I'm like, "Why do you
want to do this, man?

00:03:22.969 --> 00:03:24.437
You know, I'm going to be
bothering you

00:03:24.437 --> 00:03:26.005
for the next year and a half."

00:03:26.005 --> 00:03:28.541
I need hours and hours,
especially --

00:03:28.541 --> 00:03:31.644
You know, David is not a guy
that's used to sitting

00:03:31.644 --> 00:03:33.379
and talking
for more than 15 minutes.

00:03:33.379 --> 00:03:35.315
He's used to, "Let's go.
I got to go here.

00:03:35.315 --> 00:03:37.049
I got to promote sunglasses,
then whiskey,

00:03:37.049 --> 00:03:38.584
then this and that."

00:03:38.584 --> 00:03:40.686
And I said, "No, we're
going to do three hours,

00:03:40.686 --> 00:03:42.188
and I need time."

00:03:42.188 --> 00:03:44.757
And he was willing.
He really was.

00:03:44.757 --> 00:03:46.192
But it took, you know, hours.

00:03:46.192 --> 00:03:47.493
I was with David for over,
you know,

00:03:47.493 --> 00:03:49.662
probably about 40-plus hours.

00:03:49.662 --> 00:03:51.364
Victoria was much more
used to it.

00:03:51.364 --> 00:03:52.965
She's like, "Let's go.
I want to talk about this.

00:03:52.965 --> 00:03:55.167
I want to get it off my chest."

00:03:55.167 --> 00:03:57.236
And she was wonderful.

00:03:57.236 --> 00:04:01.474
But, yeah, I mean, look,
it's hard to go back

00:04:01.474 --> 00:04:03.276
and think about --
I mean, all of you out there,

00:04:03.276 --> 00:04:05.911
you, Seth, I say, "Remember
that time in your life,"

00:04:05.911 --> 00:04:08.414
you don't want to remember,
and you start talking about it.

00:04:08.414 --> 00:04:12.986
It's not always easy, but
I'm glad -- I'm glad he did.

00:04:12.986 --> 00:04:14.320
-And, you know,
it's interesting you say

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:16.255
it's not always easy,
because I think sometimes

00:04:16.255 --> 00:04:19.057
when someone's been around
as long as they have,

00:04:19.057 --> 00:04:22.895
you only think of all
the good fortune they've had.

00:04:22.895 --> 00:04:24.329
And this documentary shows

00:04:24.329 --> 00:04:26.198
that there were these
massive highs and lows.

00:04:26.198 --> 00:04:27.600
And it covers them,

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:29.269
and you sort of live
through them with him.

00:04:29.269 --> 00:04:31.003
There are also great,
really funny,

00:04:31.003 --> 00:04:33.172
really human mov-- moments,
excuse me,

00:04:33.172 --> 00:04:34.940
that as a filmmaker,
you must think,

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:38.076
"I can't believe
I'm catching this moment."

00:04:38.076 --> 00:04:40.046
And I want to show a clip,
but can you just --

00:04:40.046 --> 00:04:41.414
Did you know how good this was

00:04:41.414 --> 00:04:43.783
when Victoria was talking
about her upbringing

00:04:43.783 --> 00:04:47.019
and David popped his head in?

00:04:47.019 --> 00:04:49.689
-Well, I was kind of pissed off,
because I was like,

00:04:49.689 --> 00:04:52.258
"What is he doing here?"
-Yeah.

00:04:52.258 --> 00:04:55.228
-I said, "She's not there when
you're doing your interviews."

00:04:55.228 --> 00:04:57.797
So -- But I knew
it was kind of magical.

00:04:57.797 --> 00:04:59.465
And my cameraman and I
work together so much.

00:04:59.465 --> 00:05:01.266
He just like --
You'll see. They just --

00:05:01.266 --> 00:05:02.802
We flip the camera on him.

00:05:02.802 --> 00:05:05.504
But then I told him he's got to
get out of here, man, 'cause --

00:05:05.504 --> 00:05:07.540
-By the way, you know
why he had to get out of there?

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:10.810
'Cause he dropped the mic
and it was so good.

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:12.411
Let's take a look
at this wonderful clip

00:05:12.411 --> 00:05:13.512
between a married couple.

00:05:13.512 --> 00:05:15.948
-Both of our parents
work really hard.

00:05:15.948 --> 00:05:18.351
We're very working-class.

00:05:18.351 --> 00:05:19.652
-Be honest.

00:05:19.652 --> 00:05:21.788
-I am being honest.
-Be honest.

00:05:21.788 --> 00:05:24.290
-I am being honest.
-What car did your dad

00:05:24.290 --> 00:05:25.925
drive you to school in?

00:05:25.925 --> 00:05:27.993
-So, my dad --
-No, one answer.

00:05:27.993 --> 00:05:29.395
-My dad --
-What car was it?

00:05:29.395 --> 00:05:31.096
-It's not a simple answer,
because --

00:05:31.096 --> 00:05:34.100
-What car did you get your dad
to drive you to school in?

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:35.968
-It depends.
-No, no, no, no, no.

00:05:35.968 --> 00:05:38.571
-Okay, in the '80s,
my dad had a Rolls-Royce.

00:05:38.571 --> 00:05:39.806
-Thank you.

00:05:39.806 --> 00:05:41.440
[ Laughter ]

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:44.310
[ Applause ]

00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:46.412
-So good.
-I know.

00:05:46.412 --> 00:05:48.080
-You also -- I mean,

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:50.249
you interview, you know,
Sir Alex Ferguson.

00:05:50.249 --> 00:05:51.917
You interview his old teammates.

00:05:51.917 --> 00:05:55.955
You interview opponents,
former paparazzi.

00:05:55.955 --> 00:05:57.256
Most of them just have nothing

00:05:57.256 --> 00:05:59.258
but wonderful things
to say about him.

00:05:59.258 --> 00:06:02.361
And it's really -- I really
enjoy his former teammates,

00:06:02.361 --> 00:06:05.631
because they are -- a lot of
them are giving him a hard time,

00:06:05.631 --> 00:06:08.100
and they're making fun of how
much money he spent on clothes,

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:10.803
and the fact that, you know,
he spent a lot of money

00:06:10.803 --> 00:06:12.304
to buy a pen
really made me laugh.

00:06:12.304 --> 00:06:15.107
But you can tell
they just love him so much.

00:06:15.107 --> 00:06:18.043
-He's a lovable character,
I have to say.

00:06:18.043 --> 00:06:21.413
I did try to get people
to talk [bleep] about him,

00:06:21.413 --> 00:06:24.383
and nobody would.

00:06:24.383 --> 00:06:29.021
I guess Glenn Hoddle, who was
his former manager of England,

00:06:29.021 --> 00:06:31.824
he may have,
but he wouldn't talk.

00:06:31.824 --> 00:06:34.760
I tried to get Roy Keane,
who's a bruiser.

00:06:34.760 --> 00:06:37.196
He's very famous.
He was the captain.

00:06:37.196 --> 00:06:39.631
I said, "Roy, when he showed up,
you know,

00:06:39.631 --> 00:06:40.933
with these haircuts
and wearing --

00:06:40.933 --> 00:06:42.601
and when he was in a sarong,

00:06:42.601 --> 00:06:44.771
weren't you, like, upset
that he was, like, taking --"

00:06:44.771 --> 00:06:46.872
He said, "I loved him,
I loved him."

00:06:46.872 --> 00:06:50.242
I'm like, geez, so I couldn't
get anybody to talk --

00:06:50.242 --> 00:06:52.745
Yeah, but he's a lovable guy.

00:06:52.745 --> 00:06:54.346
-It's interesting
because I wonder

00:06:54.346 --> 00:06:58.016
if you found,
in doing this exercise,

00:06:58.016 --> 00:07:00.586
there's these wonderful shots
where you are filming him

00:07:00.586 --> 00:07:02.254
and other players
watching themselves.

00:07:02.254 --> 00:07:04.489
-Yeah.
-And you get the sense

00:07:04.489 --> 00:07:06.024
that these athletes --

00:07:06.024 --> 00:07:08.694
and obviously,
I guess having lived it,

00:07:08.694 --> 00:07:12.064
they don't feel much need to go
back and watch old highlights.

00:07:12.064 --> 00:07:13.933
-Yeah.
-And so you not only realize,

00:07:13.933 --> 00:07:16.035
"Oh, I'm watching him
watch this play,"

00:07:16.035 --> 00:07:18.037
but he maybe hasn't watched it
since it happened.

00:07:18.037 --> 00:07:21.274
-Yeah, with the exception of the
Greece -- famous Greece goal,

00:07:21.274 --> 00:07:24.609
which he had watched recently,
he hadn't seen himself play.

00:07:24.609 --> 00:07:26.579
I showed him --

00:07:26.579 --> 00:07:28.981
There's a clip where I show him
his first appearance.

00:07:28.981 --> 00:07:30.516
He's 17 years old.

00:07:30.516 --> 00:07:33.752
He comes out like a little fawn,
like he could barely walk.

00:07:33.752 --> 00:07:36.121
And he hadn't seen that --

00:07:36.121 --> 00:07:38.257
I don't think he'd ever
seen that or -- you know?

00:07:38.257 --> 00:07:40.225
And it blew his mind, right?

00:07:40.225 --> 00:07:42.494
And he's so vulnerable
and he's looking at it

00:07:42.494 --> 00:07:44.663
and he's, like,
getting emotional.

00:07:44.663 --> 00:07:49.201
And Eric Cantona,
a legendary French player,

00:07:49.201 --> 00:07:53.405
hadn't obviously watched him
play -- himself play for years.

00:07:53.405 --> 00:07:56.175
And just to get those --
those expressions,

00:07:56.175 --> 00:07:58.176
that vulnerability
of these tough,

00:07:58.176 --> 00:08:01.313
hard athletes being
like little children again

00:08:01.313 --> 00:08:03.749
was sort of what we were after,
and especially David,

00:08:03.749 --> 00:08:07.219
because just to see into
his soul for that brief moment

00:08:07.219 --> 00:08:08.521
was sort of what we were after.

00:08:08.521 --> 00:08:09.721
-There's a real wonderful moment

00:08:09.721 --> 00:08:11.624
because, again,
I think of Eric Cantona

00:08:11.624 --> 00:08:14.193
as this very
sort of hard player.

00:08:14.193 --> 00:08:17.029
This guy did not
show much emotion

00:08:17.029 --> 00:08:19.098
other than,
it seemed like, rage.

00:08:19.098 --> 00:08:20.399
-Yes.
Yeah.

00:08:20.399 --> 00:08:23.235
-And you tell him --
'cause you show that clip

00:08:23.235 --> 00:08:24.503
from the first game
and you say, you know,

00:08:24.503 --> 00:08:26.405
"David said the best moment
about his first goal

00:08:26.405 --> 00:08:27.940
was he got to run over
and hug you,"

00:08:27.940 --> 00:08:30.943
and you watch it land
on Eric Cantona.

00:08:30.943 --> 00:08:32.545
And he just goes, "He did?"
-Yeah.

00:08:32.545 --> 00:08:34.847
-And it's so --
it's a really wonderful moment.

00:08:34.847 --> 00:08:36.448
-Yeah.
-I want to finish

00:08:36.448 --> 00:08:38.350
because, you know, we started
by talking about SAG.

00:08:38.350 --> 00:08:40.352
I did not realize this
until today.

00:08:40.352 --> 00:08:43.088
You were not born
Fisher Stevens.

00:08:43.088 --> 00:08:45.090
And SAG is the reason
you are Fisher Stevens.

00:08:45.090 --> 00:08:47.793
-Right, my name --
I was born Mike Johnson.

00:08:47.793 --> 00:08:49.228
[ Laughter ]

00:08:49.228 --> 00:08:50.229
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:50.229 --> 00:08:52.365
And it was like...

00:08:52.365 --> 00:08:55.568
There was a guy named
Mike Johnson.

00:08:55.568 --> 00:08:57.770
-Guess what.
You just did your Rolls-Royce.

00:08:57.770 --> 00:08:59.605
That's real good.

00:08:59.605 --> 00:09:02.842
-No, no,
I was born Steven Fisher,

00:09:02.842 --> 00:09:06.011
and when I got my SAG card,

00:09:06.011 --> 00:09:08.748
and the producers of the movie
that I had just done called me

00:09:08.748 --> 00:09:11.016
and said, "You can't use
your name, Steven Fisher.

00:09:11.016 --> 00:09:12.951
You need to either
put a middle name in there

00:09:12.951 --> 00:09:14.486
or change your name."

00:09:14.486 --> 00:09:17.422
And everybody called me "Fish."
My dad called me Fish.

00:09:17.422 --> 00:09:19.058
And there was a sign
that I used to see.

00:09:19.058 --> 00:09:24.263
It said Fisher-Stevens Stoves
in Brooklyn on this building.

00:09:24.263 --> 00:09:26.865
And I got the idea, I'll just
reverse it -- Fisher Stevens.

00:09:26.865 --> 00:09:30.836
But I changed the Stephen
from S-T-E-P-H-E-N

00:09:30.836 --> 00:09:32.038
to S-T-E-V-E-N-S.

00:09:32.038 --> 00:09:35.207
But, yeah, so let's get back
to work, SAG.

00:09:35.207 --> 00:09:36.475
Come on!
-Let's go.

00:09:36.475 --> 00:09:39.445
We got Fisher Stevens
who's chomping at the bit.

00:09:39.445 --> 00:09:41.346
"Beckham" is streaming now
on Netflix.

00:09:41.346 --> 00:09:42.548
It's fantastic.

00:09:42.548 --> 00:09:44.216
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

